From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mark Harfouche <mark.harfouche@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 09:22:34 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Replace nnets/base.py's tf.compat.v1 losses, and stop the
 eager fixture skipping later modules

Two things, both in the files this agent owns.

1. nnets/losses.py

`BasePoseNet.train()` built its three terms with `tf.compat.v1.losses.
sigmoid_cross_entropy`, `.huber_loss` and `.mean_squared_error` -- five
references across four call sites, the last of the losses' TF1 dependencies.
They are replaced by a small module that reproduces them exactly.

The reduction is the entire difficulty.  `tf.compat.v1.losses.*` default to
`Reduction.SUM_BY_NONZERO_WEIGHTS`, which divides by the number of elements the
weight tensor is non-zero at after broadcasting -- not by the number of
elements.  DeepLabCut passes a 0/1 mask as `weights` for its locref and
pairwise terms, so the denominator there is the count of unmasked elements, and
`tf.reduce_mean` is not a substitute.

How wrong it would be depends on the project, which is what makes it a trap.
Measured on the real batches `PoseDatasetFactory` produces:

  single animal, "default"        locref_mask non-zero on 0.15% of elements
                                  v1 0.559652865, reduce_mean 0.000833985
                                  -- 671x too small
  multi-animal, "multi-animal-imgaug"
                                  locref_mask and pairwise_mask non-zero on
                                  100.00% -- reduce_mean agrees exactly

The maDLC loader is dense by configuration rather than by nature:
`pose_multianimal_imgaug` builds `np.ones(locref_size)` unless
`weigh_only_present_joints`, which defaults to False.  So a `reduce_mean`
implementation validated on a stock multi-animal project agrees to the last
digit and then scales the locref term down by nearly three orders of magnitude
on every single-animal project -- training still runs, the loss still falls,
and only the balance between the score-map term and the locref term is wrong.
The part-detection term agrees either way, because its weights are the scalar
1.0 under the default `weigh_part_predictions: False`.  There is a test that
fails if anyone later "simplifies" the reduction to `reduce_mean`.

Verification drives the real `train()` body twice on the same fixed head
tensors, once through nnets/losses.py and once with the module globals swapped
back to `tf.compat.v1.losses.*`, so nothing is reimplemented for the check:

  8 real single-animal batches, huber and mean_squared_error
      |d value| 0.0 on every term of every batch
      |d gradient| w.r.t. the heads <= 3.7e-09
  8 real multi-animal + PAF batches, huber and mean_squared_error
      |d value| 0.0, |d gradient| <= 9.1e-13
  edge cases the real batches do not reach, in graph mode and in eager mode:
      sparse, fractional and trailing-1-broadcast part_score_weights;
      all-zero locref and pairwise masks; intermediate supervision
      |d value| 0.0 on all of them, and the 0/0 masks give 0.0, not nan

The one behavioural difference is that the v1 helpers also append the loss to
`GraphKeys.LOSSES`.  Nothing in DeepLabCut reads that collection -- there is no
`get_total_loss`, `get_losses` or `get_regularization_losses` anywhere in the
package -- so dropping the side effect changes nothing.  Relatedly, and for the
same reason, the eager trainer must not add `model.losses` to the tape: the
weight-decay regularizers `layers.prediction_layer` registers have never been
summed into the loss either.

`prediction_layers`' `tf.compat.v1.variable_scope` is deliberately left alone.
Patching out just that one call site and dumping the variable names shows why:
replacing it with `tf.name_scope`, the usual TF2 substitution, leaves the
variable count at 271 but renames all six head keys from
`pose/part_pred/block4/weights` to `part_pred/block4/weights`.  Every snapshot
DeepLabCut has written is keyed by the first form, and `export.py` reads
`pose/locref_pred/block4/BiasAdd` and `pose/pairwise_pred/block4/BiasAdd` out
of the frozen graph by name, so a restore with `allow_missing` would silently
load nothing.  It belongs to the tf_slim nets and goes when they do; the Keras
path never reaches it, passing the scope to `keras_prediction_layer` as a
string.

That leaves two `tf.compat.v1` references in nnets/base.py: that
`variable_scope`, and `enable_eager_execution` in `ensure_eager_execution`.

2. tests/test_nnets_keras.py's eager fixture

The fixture disabled eager again on teardown, to leave the flag as it found it.
That cannot work: eager can be turned back on only until the eager context has
been touched, so the flag only ever flips one way.  Whichever Keras test module
ran second therefore had `ensure_eager_execution` raise, and this fixture turns
that into a skip -- so an entire module's tests were reported as skipped rather
than failed, and nothing looked wrong.  Eager is now left on.

Since patch 0020 moved `disable_eager_execution` out of `core/train.py`'s import
and into `core/legacy_tf1.ensure_graph_mode`, eager is on by default and the
teardown no longer fires, so the cascade does not reproduce on a plain run.  It
returns as soon as anything enters graph mode first.  Making that explicit --
calling `ensure_graph_mode()` and then running the two Keras modules in one
pytest session, this module first:

  before   61 passed, 25 skipped
  after    81 passed,  4 skipped

The 4 remaining skips are parametrisations with no Keras implementation yet.

The two docstrings that still described the old import-time
`disable_eager_execution` are corrected, in `ensure_eager_execution` and in
tests/test_nnets_keras_equivalence.py, along with the error message advising
separate processes.  Both engines can now share a process in either order --
checked by writing a snapshot from the Keras net and restoring it into the
tf_slim graph, agreeing to 3.7e-06 relative with the Keras engine built first
and 3.1e-06 with graph mode entered first.  The equivalence tests keep their
subprocess per engine anyway: eager is per-process global state, and isolating
it is what makes that module independent of collection order.

Full TensorFlow test suite: 2511 passed, 5 skipped, 5 xfailed.  The three
failures that remain -- `test_pose_net_leaves_the_caller_config_alone` and the
two `[*-efficientnet-b0]` config parametrisations -- are present unchanged on
the shipped tree before this patch and are not addressed here; see the report.
